Cambodia plans to import more Arabica coffee with organic standards and clear sources from Laos
(Phnom Penh): While the demand for organic coffee in Cambodia is growing, Cambodia plans to import more Arabica coffee from Champasak province, Laos, but those coffees must comply with CEDAC organic standards, which are gaining credibility from For coffee lovers and coffee with a clear source.
In the case of Champasak Province (adjacent to Stung Treng Province, Cambodia), this province has an altitude of 1,294 meters. The climate is cold. The specialty coffee is Arabica coffee.
It should be noted that there are two types of coffee that you drink every day: Arabica coffee (high in caffeine and strong in cold and high climates) and Robusta coffee (low in caffeine and weaker than Arabica coffee).
During a three-day visit to Champasak province from December 26-29, 2018, a team from CEDAC and a group of Cambodian journalists met with a large Lao coffee plantation community in Paksong district, Champa province. Sak, which has more than 2,400 members as farmers.
In a large coffee plantation full of coffee trees mixed with lush green plants, Oum Sabunsay Borkeo, also a member of the coffee community, said that there are five other subtypes of Arabica coffee. These include: Saudi Arabia, Turkey, Timor-Leste, Saudi Arabia, Kathmandu, Saudi Arabia and Saudi Arabia.
Mr. Sabun said that in his community, they like to grow Arabica coffee and Arabica coffee, both of which can be grown together in the same field because the geographical conditions there are very favorable for both types of coffee.
After a long discussion, the CEDAC team replied that CEDAC wanted to further expand the coffee market in Cambodia because the demand for organic coffee in Cambodia is currently increasing while the local organic coffee can not meet the demand. No.
CEDAC representative stated that CEDAC chose Champasak province, Laos for the purchase of organic coffee to meet local demand because this province has a special gift for coffee. In addition, Lao farmers there also use less chemicals for their coffee, which makes it easier to convert their coffee into organic coffee in accordance with CEDAC standards.
Ms. Tong Chantheang, Executive Director of CEDAC, stated that it will take at least three years for the conversion of coffee plantations from natural cultivation to coffee cultivation in accordance with organic standards.
She said that the help of Lao farmers to grow organic coffee will start in 2019, and CEDAC will regularly inspect the cultivation of Lao farmers to certify the organic standards to Lao farmers before importing. Hold Cambodia.
Lao farmers have shown a strong willingness to cooperate with CEDAC Cambodia to convert their coffee into organic coffee with organic certification and export to the Cambodian market.
Please be informed that in Cambodia, most of them are rich in Robusta coffee, which is grown according to CEDAC organic standards in Mondulkiri province, such as in Busra commune, Pichreda district and Dak Dam commune, Oreang district, which is only about 900 meters high, while Arabica coffee is not yet There are many more yet.
