Nearly 250,000 people have diabetes in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Prof. Dr. Koy Vanny, Hormone Specialist and President of the Cambodian Diabetes and Endocrinologists Association, said that Cambodia has 246,000 people with diabetes (2.6% prevalence rate). Compared to the number of adults).
"This has had a profound effect on the health economy in Cambodia and around the world," he said.
Dr. Thil Sophea, a endocrinologist at the Khmer-Soviet Friendship Hospital, also said that diabetes is a deadly disease that can cause complications such as eye damage, kidney damage, impotence and incurable foot ulcers. Yes.
"To prevent these complications, diabetics need to control their blood sugar and blood pressure consistently, along with a proper diet, regular exercise and prescription medication," he added.
