Such a statement by Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng was made on the afternoon of December 27, 2018 at the closing ceremony of the workshop on the preparation of the action plan and budget for 2019 of the NCDD. The workshop was held at the Ministry of Interior and was attended by representatives of ministries, sub-national administrative institutions, development partners and relevant civil society organizations.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng emphasized that the provision of administrative services through the OWSO mechanism implemented in the past, despite some shortcomings, has yielded positive results and has received support and active participation from all parties. All stakeholders, in particular, have received great satisfaction and trust from the people who receive and use the service.
Samdech Krala Hom added, "In this regard, I would like to request the NCDD Secretariat, General Department of Administration, Ministry of Interior to continue to cooperate with relevant ministries and institutions and capital-provincial administrations to support the strengthening and expansion of the mechanism. This one-stop shop for district and commune administrations that do not yet have this mechanism as soon as possible.
According to Sar Kheng, the implementation and implementation of this OWSO has provided several important benefits, including:
First, strengthen the responsibilities of sub-national administrations in managing the provision of administrative services effectively, responding in a timely manner to the needs and requests of the people.
Second, it has made it easier for people to come and receive administrative services in one place that is easy to find, and there is a clear definition of the duration and cost of each service.
Third, make it easier for people to monitor the process of providing administrative services of sub-national administrations.
And fourth, strengthen the collection of revenue from the provision of these administrative services in a transparent manner and prevent corruption.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng also said that although these services are concentrated in the OWSO of the Capital-Provincial Administration, each department still has a central role in reviewing and advising. Technical aspects of the provision of these services, as well as monitoring and monitoring the implementation of those services.
