In 2011, the United Arab Emirates recalled its ambassador from Syria following the start of a revolution against the government of Syrian President Assad, which plunged the Middle East country into civil war. The same year, Syria was also expelled from the 22-nation Arab League, with Arab countries sanctioning and condemning Damascus for using military force against dissidents.
With the reopening of the embassy, the United Arab Emirates became the first member of the Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) to resume diplomatic relations with Syria. This comes after the December 16 visit to Damascus by Sudanese President Omar Bashir, who became the first Arab head of state to visit Syria since the outbreak of civil war.
Nearly eight years after the conflict began, Assad has occupied large swathes of Syrian territory with the help of Russia and Iran.
