Minister of Health: Providing health care services to citizens, civil servants through the Equity Fund and the National Social Security Fund is a facilitation of financial barriers
(Siem Reap): Mr. Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, stated that the provision of health care services to workers, civil servants, the disabled, retirees, workers in the system and outside the system, etc. through the Equity Fund and the National Social Security Fund. Caring for poor pregnant women is the policy of the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, to facilitate financial barriers for citizens.
The Minister of Health said this on December 24, 2018 while working at the Siem Reap Provincial Referral Hospital and two other health centers in Siem Reap.
Mr. Mam Bunheng said, "This is a mission to monitor the quality of public health services to the people and monitor the operation of the Equity Fund and the provision of the National Social Security Fund, as well as to encourage all health officials and doctors. "Skills in all areas need to pay more attention to the care of the people sincerely with all their physical and mental strength."
He added that in mid-2019, poor pregnant women with poverty certificates issued by local authorities and the Ministry of Planning will receive free pregnancy tests. Substitute payers and even receive a stipend when receiving prenatal check-ups four times, each time receiving support from the Royal Government through the Equity Fund of 40,000 Riels.
Separately, at the time of delivery, received a subsidy of 200,000 Riels. After giving birth, poor women are supported by the Equity Fund for 10 health check-ups for both mother and child under the additional support of 40,000 Riels each time. The total number of poor women who received support before, during, and after childbirth was 760,000 riels, approximately $ 190, and did not have to pay any other fees.
At the end of the year, China usually imports most of its soybeans from the United States, which is mainly used for livestock feed, and the United States is China's second largest supplier of soybeans from year to year. The partnership was worth about $ 12 billion last year.
But in July, Beijing raised tariffs on US imports by 25 percent in response to a US tariff increase on Chinese goods. Since then, US soybean imports to China have declined until a complete halt in November. Instead, China bought 5.07 million tonnes of soybeans from Brazil last month, an increase of more than 80 percent from 2017.
