(Ethiopia): The railway connecting Ethiopia and Djibouti, built with the help of China, has reduced the cost of transportation from the port of Djibouti to the landlocked country of Ethiopia. For Isuzu, the railway serves as an important artery for the future economic development of the northeastern part of Africa.
Since January, the railway has helped alleviate the problems that businesses here have encountered. This single-track freight train can carry between 3,500 and 4,000 tons per leg. And the Ethiopian government expects the railway to carry 7 million tons of cargo a year.
The United Nations Development Program (UNDP) in Ethiopia has released a report entitled "Industrialization with People". The report acknowledges that the transnational railway plays an important role in Ethiopia's economic development.
In addition to linking the two countries, many economists say the railway project is a driving force for economic growth in Africa as a whole.
Representatives of Carabao claim that their products are of good quality and contain no harmful ingredients!
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Huy Sokha, Marketing Manager of Carabao Company, has completely dismissed the previous case in which some social media outlets spread false information, claiming that drinking Carabao drink is harmful to health and even life. He claimed that the information was not true.
"All the information that has been posted on Facebook in the past that says that drinking carabao has a serious health risk to the point of death is untrue," the company representative said.
"Carabao is not new to the Cambodian market and has received a lot of support from customers," he said. "But lately, some packs have been spreading false information to confuse customers. Therefore, Carabao claims that all of its products with Khmer characters are legal products that are properly recognized by the Ministry."
Mr. Huy Sokha, Marketing Manager of the company, added that, therefore, I would like to ask all of you and your customers to continue to have faith in Carabao. In particular, Paks who spread false information will face legal action in the near future.
