The inscription "Sovannaphum", the most important evidence of Cambodia, was taken to the National Museum after being found in Kampong Speu province
(Kampong Speu): Ancient Khmer inscriptions from the Chenla period during the 7th century, inscribed with the word "Sovannaphum" and the most important heritage and evidence of the Cambodian nation, were paraded to the National Museum in Phnom Penh. Safe after receiving from monks and people in Bor Seth district, Kampong Speu province.
The procession of important inscriptions to the National Museum was conducted by the Kampong Speu Provincial Administration on December 21, 2018, with the presence of Buddhists, monks and many government officials.
The inscription above is one of the most important of the 36 ancient Khmer inscriptions with the inscription "Sovannaphum". The inscription was originally found in Bor Seth district, Kampong Speu province, and was kept at Wat Kiri Sdach Kong in Svay Chacheb commune, Bor Seth district, and was recently hidden by the Chief Inspector of Wat Kiri Steng Kong. This (authorities request to be kept in the museum.
This is because the monks and the people there believe that since the inscription was kept in Wat Kiri Sdech Kong, the land there was cold and the people always prayed for the inscription as Such a sacred object.
Why is this inscription considered such a tremendous treasure? The point is explained as follows:
However, an expert on Khmer inscriptions, Mr. Vong Sotheara, claimed that he found an ancient Khmer inscription in 633 AD. The inscription reads, "Sovannaphum is under the control of a Khmer king named Isanvarman (I), who was found at Wat Kiri, King Kong, also known as Wat Boeung, Bor Seth commune and Svay Chab commune, Bor Seth district, Kampong Speu province on the 22nd. December 2017 recently.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch urges all leaders, officials and staff of the Mine Authority to focus on action planning, priorities and proper use of the organization's available resources in 2019
(Kep): On Saturday, December 15, 2018, Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Deputy Chairman of the Mine Authority, presided over the closing ceremony of the Vipassana Meditation on Capacity Development. Officials of the Cambodian Authority control mine action and rescue of people with disabilities due to landmines.
On that occasion, the Senior Minister said that during the two-day Vipassana, he really provided a lot of new experiences and knowledge to the officials and staff of the Mine Authority to carry out their work effectively and efficiently.
The Senior Minister requested the leadership, officials and staff to pay attention:
First, resource factors (human and financial resources); second, to prioritize action plans for the organization; and third, there must be proper procedures for managing and implementing activities effectively and efficiently.
Kandal Provincial Council Association Holds Training Course on "Global Principles of Governance and Good Governance" for 800 Municipal, District, Commune and Sangkat Councils
(Kandal): The Association of Municipal, District, Commune and Sangkat Councils of Kandal Province on December 22, 2018 started its training course on "Universal Principles of Governance and Good Governance" to orient the members of Municipal / District Council About 800 communes, starting the first session in Kien Svay district with the participation of 70 district councilors, commune chiefs and commune councilors.
One-day training course was held at Kien Svay District Hall for 70 district councilors, commune chiefs and commune councilors from 8 communes of Kien Svay district.
Kong Chheng, President of the Association of Municipal, District, Commune and Sangkat Councils of Kandal Province, said that the training on both lessons was aimed at enhancing the capacity of district councilors and commune councilors on the policy of democratic development. The Royal Government of Cambodia will reflect on practical implementation at the sub-national level.
Mr. Kong Chheng emphasized that the lessons taught by the association were organized by national and international experts with the support of EU-UNDP in Cambodia. Lesson content includes: good society, good governance, social accountability, decentralization and deconcentration in Cambodia, roles and responsibilities of sub-national administrative councils and planning of municipal, district and khan councils and commune councils.
Mr. Kong Chheng stated that the association will continue such courses in 10 other districts, with the second session to be held on December 24, 2018 in Khsach Kandal district to Khsach Kandal district councilors, commune chiefs and group members. A total of 90 commune councilors from 18 communes in Khsach Kandal district.
The Association of Municipal, District, Commune and Sangkat Councils of Kandal Province was established with the aim of enhancing the status and capacity of the Council in order to provide effective services to its members.
Currently, 11 district councils and 127 commune councils throughout Kandal province are members of the Association of Municipal, District, Commune and Sangkat Councils of Kandal Province.
