By: Political observers, coffee table, a new type of medical food made from fish to treat children suffering from severe malnutrition!
At the launch ceremony on December 17, 2018 at the Nutrix Bakery in Phnom Penh, all partners announced that the instant treats will be mass-produced in response to Child malnutrition in Cambodia.
Professor Mam Bunheng, Minister of Health, said, "The announcement of the launch of this innovative local therapeutic food is an important step towards achieving the Royal Government's goal of at least treating children." "25,000 people a year are suffering from acute malnutrition."
"The launch of this therapeutic diet will help ensure local products for the treatment of acute malnutrition," he added. "Over the last five years, many partners have helped us develop this product, and we hope that this new partnership will enable us to extend the reach of treatment to children suffering from acute malnutrition." .
In Cambodia, about 2.6 percent of children are diagnosed with acute malnutrition. To treat these children, immediately available treats are given to children to take home, and without such food many children may still need to be hospitalized.
However, research shows that children in need of treatment can receive only a small amount of this food from health institutions in Cambodia.
