Three-star General Di Vichea: Cambodia asks Laos to extradite a Cambodian mastermind in a case of fraud of thousands of people worth more than 60 million dollars
(Phnom Penh): In early December 2018, Cambodia submitted a request for extradition to the Lao People's Democratic Republic to send a Cambodian accused to be sentenced within the jurisdiction of the Kingdom of Cambodia, according to a senior Cambodian police official. Arrest Warrant No. 427, dated 11 July 2017 of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court.
Lt. Gen. Di Vichea, Deputy Commissioner of the National Police and Director of the Central Security Department of the National Police Commission of the Ministry of Interior, on December 17, 2018, told Fresh News that the leadership of the Ministry of Interior and the National Police Commission are Do your best, both legally and diplomatically, to bring the accused from Laos and to bring justice to the innocent people.
"The competent authorities are continuing to search and investigate further to find out and bring the main ringleaders and members of this group to justice according to the law, no matter how long it takes," he said.
A senior Cambodian official also confirmed today that Cambodia is pushing for extradition proceedings with the Lao People's Democratic Republic in the spirit of good neighborliness.
It should be noted that the Lao People's Democratic Republic and the Kingdom of Cambodia signed the extradition treaty in 1999, which came into force in 2005. At the same time, within the framework of the 10 ASEAN countries, there is also a treaty on mutual legal aid in criminal matters. Health Minister: Severely malnourished children face high risk of death
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Health, Professor Mam Bunheng, stated that children with acute malnutrition face a high risk of death due to common childhood diseases such as diarrhea and pneumonia.
This statement was made by the Minister of Health on the occasion of presiding over the inauguration ceremony of the factory to produce food for children with severe malnutrition on December 17, 2018.
"Nutritional factors contribute to about 30% of child deaths under the age of five, and in Cambodia from the early 2000s to the present, the Ministry of Health has provided medical care to about five children with severe malnutrition," he said. "Out of about 90,000 children, about 6% need treatment."
Ms. Vivianna Belmonte said that through the official inauguration of the factory for the treatment of children with severe malnutrition today, Cambodia is the second country in the region to produce its own food for treatment. Available immediately.
She added that the Royal Government of Cambodia can provide Nutrix, an affordable snack treat for children under the age of five who are suffering from severe malnutrition in remote and impoverished communities. Local health center term. In Cambodia, it is estimated that between 6-90,000 children a year need special treatment, such as diet therapy.
