China suspends tariffs on US cars, parts for three months from next year
BEIJING (Reuters) - The Chinese government announced on Friday that it would suspend tariffs on US cars and parts for three months. This is according to a quote from the Chinese newspaper CGTN on Friday night, December 14, 2018.
From January 1 to March 31, 2019, China will suspend tariffs of 25 percent on a total of 144 cars and parts imported from the United States, according to a statement from the Chinese Foreign Ministry. Beijing hopes the two countries will be able to speed up talks to completely eliminate any tariff increases on each other's goods.
The announcement comes after US and Chinese President Xi Jinping agreed to ease tensions over a 90-day trade war during an informal meeting on the sidelines of the G20 summit. Held in Argentina earlier this month.
