(China): Chinese films produced by small, independent companies are gaining a foothold in the Chinese film production market, a sector that relies heavily on large budgets for filming. And in recent years, cinemas as small art galleries are also booming.
Movies produced by small, independent companies, or so-called independent films, have been underfunded or unpopular in China for decades. In fact, most Chinese independent films are only screened on International Day and not in China. But the situation is changing.
A new wave of independent filmmakers in China is looking for ways to seize the opportunities available in today's fast-growing Chinese film market. Producers have the right to submit their films to the Screening and Editing Committee so that they can raise money from investors for filming, with the ultimate goal of attracting more viewers. .
Some mini-movies have been hugely successful due to the large number of viewers.
"China is opening up the market and welcoming new stories with new content," said David Lee, vice president of the Singapore Film Association. "Because the Chinese movie market is so large, viewers are always looking for something new, unique and diverse."
As China's film industry is gaining momentum, audiences' tastes are changing. With more and more viewers wanting to see good movies, independent filmmakers are likely to have a higher chance of success in terms of both fame and revenue in China. (China's CCTV)
