New study: Shark numbers declining on Australia's east coast
(Sydney) - While unofficial evidence appears to be on the rise in sharks off Australia's east coast, a new scientific study released on Friday said that the numbers are actually increasing. But it declined rapidly. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Friday afternoon, December 14, 2018.
The findings come from more than five decades of data analysis based on the Queensland Shark Management Program, established in 1962. In stark contrast to the general opinion of most Australians that shark numbers are on the rise, this finding is likely to come as a big surprise to Australians themselves.
While it is not entirely clear why shark populations are declining so quickly, researchers believe it is largely due to overfishing and insisted on precautionary measures. These fish because of their importance in the ecosystem of the ocean.
Representative of the Minister of Social Affairs: The rehabilitation sector in Cambodia has enabled people with disabilities to improve their lives and increase their rights in Cambodian society.
