In Phnom Penh, the National Blood Transfusion Center is responsible for supplying more than 70%, while the provinces have only 1-5%
(Phnom Penh): The President of the National Blood Transfusion Center, Mr. Hok Kim Cheng, said that in Phnom Penh, the National Blood Transfusion Center is responsible for more than 70% of the blood supply, while the responsibility in the provinces is only 1% to 5%. Only because most patients who need blood are sent to Phnom Penh, that's why the percentage in Phnom Penh is higher than in the provinces.
At the IU Student Voluntary Blood Donation Ceremony at the International University (IU) on December 13, 2018, Mr. Hok Kim Cheng stated that the National Blood Transfusion Center has partnered with the Love Club. Of the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia in organizing a campaign at all universities in order to collect blood donations to save the lives of people hospitalized in all hospitals.
"Especially in Phnom Penh, the National Blood Transfusion Center is responsible for about 70 percent of the country's blood supply," he said.
He added that in the provinces, there are 21 national centers, and the National Blood Transfusion Centers in the provinces are responsible for supplying and providing services to the people in the provinces without the need for people to come to Phnom Penh for services. Also.
The director of the National Blood Transfusion Center stressed that people in the provinces have less blood consumption (only 1%, 2% and 5%). He said that the National Blood Transfusion Center is responsible for up to 70 percent of the country.
He added that this is a serious burden of the National Blood Transfusion Center for the blood supply to save the lives of people who come to receive treatment at national hospitals and other private hospitals according to the needs of Pol as well.
He added that the development of the National Blood Transfusion Center is still a lot, which requires the Center to find partners such as: the Cambodian Red Cross and the Union of Youth Federations of Cambodia in advocating for a polio to ensure. Sustainability in this much-developed blood supply.
He added that the provision of blood supply is a humanitarian service, which is currently the sole responsibility of the Royal Government as well as the Ministry of Health, with all blood components being supplied to hospitals across the country without paying for services. No, with the state as the insurer.
Please be reminded that in 2017, the National Blood Transfusion Center received 76,511 blood donations nationwide. In particular, in 2018, blood donations received and supplied to 83,984 patients across the country, including at the National Center in Phnom Penh, up to 58,379 units.
