(Kampong Speu): A valuable Khmer inscription, a great pavilion built before the Mohanakor period (7th century) with the most important inscription "Sovannaphum" was found on the 10th This December 2018, after the Chief Inspector of Wat Kiri Steng Kong agreed to hand over to the authorities to be kept as a national heritage property at the National Museum in Phnom Penh.
The inscription above is one of the 36 major inscriptions bearing the inscription "Sovannaphum". This inscription was found in Bor Seth district, Kampong Speu province and kept at Wat Kiri Sdach Kong in Svay Chacheb commune, Bor Seth district.
In connection with this great treasure, it was originally very worrying for the whole Cambodian nation because the Chief Inspector of Wat Kiri Steng Kong hid the inscription underground (in the sense of preserving protection). Security inscriptions) as well as mobilize Buddhists to prevent the authorities and law enforcement forces from searching for the hidden inscriptions.
Mr. Horm Kimson, Deputy Director General of Heritage of the Ministry of Culture, and Mr. Sor Soputra, Deputy Governor of Kampong Speu Province, confirmed that the authority requested the inscription from Wat Kiri Sdech Kong in order to bring this valuable property to be kept as a national heritage property in The National Museum is both safe and useful for the Cambodian nation.
However, the pagoda's chief priest and the people refused to hand over the inscription to the authorities, and even hid the inscription, causing great concern from the public and the entire Cambodian nation for the safety of the inscription. This Khmer poster.
Due to the coordination of the provincial leadership and by Samdech Preah Sang Neay Non Nget to the Chief Bishop of Wat Kiri Sdach Kong, only on December 10, 2018, the Chief Bishop of Wat Kiri King Kong agreed to hand over the inscription to the authorities.
Meanwhile, the leaders of Kampong Speu province and the police are preparing to bring the inscription to be kept at the National Museum of Phnom Penh to clear doubts to the public and to strengthen the security of this national heritage.
