In addition, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration adheres to the principles of human rights and public freedom of the people, especially the right to freedom of expression, peaceful assembly, but these rights are under the law without local authorities as mediators and balance. The rights of protesters and the rights of others for the greater good of the nation and the principles of public order. Mr. Khuong Sreng added.
Mr. Khuong Sreng continued that the celebration of the 70th anniversary of the International Human Rights Day on December 10 under the theme "Together to keep the peace to promote human rights" is while the Royal Government of Cambodia has been preparing for a major historical event. In Phnom Penh, the inauguration of the Victory Political Memorial and the celebration of the 20th anniversary of the end of Cambodia's civil war.
The President of the Senate highly appreciated and commended the Royal Government under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, for ending the civil war in Cambodia in a win-win policy in 1998 and preventing the color revolution movement in time. Who attempted to create a war in Cambodia and brought peace and development in all fields to the motherland as it is today.
The President of the Senate also said that democracy, the rule of law, the development and respect for human rights came from a peaceful, politically stable Cambodia, a noble historical achievement that comes from the great struggles and sacrifices of the people. The state and the people.
In the wake of the 2011 protests in Tunisia, Western analysts and professors have described Facebook as a manifestation of the democratic process and a messenger of democratic freedoms across Africa. North and the Middle East, and Facebook has even raised expectations for the success of the political uprising.
In late 2011, Facebook played a key role in organizing large-scale demonstrations in Russia after a parliamentary election that was claimed to have been rigged. At the time, a similar pattern was observed between the protests in Tunisia and the protests in Russia, with no clear leader-led protest network emerging from US social media.
This has led Russian President Vladimir Putin to suspect that the United States has organized such activities in different parts of the world using the same approach. But Putin, along with other observers, was seen as mistaken in believing that Facebook's role in these notorious insurgency was linked to freedom or democracy.
Meanwhile, the unrest is happening again in France, a country that can not be considered a dictatorship and where the United States has no reason to instigate a revolution. The so-called "yellow vest" protests erupted with the French government's decision to raise taxes by 7.6 percent on gasoline and 3.9 percent on diesel.
Given the rate of tax hikes, it may not seem like a big deal, but the yellow vest protests that erupted in November as a result of posting inspiring messages on Facebook have escalated into violent protests. The most downtown area in France in more than a decade.
However, whether the rage of the French people caused by the tax increase is real or at least partly related to the Facebook inspiration is difficult to determine for now. Without any clear scientific method. But it should be time to let go of any illusions that social media can play a positive role in promoting democracy and freedom.
