(Anlong Veng, Oddar Meanchey): Although Cambodian territory was liberated from the Khmer Rouge regime on January 7, 1979 by the United Front for National Salvation, the territory of Anlong Veng district as well as the people in that area have to wait. It took almost 20 more years to be liberated and to have the same rights and freedoms as citizens in other areas.
Former Khmer leaders, after the overthrow of their regime, fled to the Thai-Cambodian border, especially in Anlong Veng district. Many important former leaders went into hiding in Anlong Veng district, such as Pol Pot Sun Sen and Ung Choeun alias Ta Mok.
Under the leadership of the former Khmer Rouge leader, the people of Anlong Veng are still living under the blockade of all kinds of freedoms, no private property, no spending of paper money, and many other persecutions, especially the hardships of the shooting. Almost daily shelling from government forces and Khmer Rouge forces.
Prak Touch, 57, of Tuol Sala village, Trapeang Prey commune, Anlong Veng district, is a resident of Anlong Veng district since the late 1970s, when the area was ruled by a former Khmer Rouge leader. Ms. Prak Touch clearly remembers the difficult living conditions at that time.
Kanh Khean, Prak Touch's husband, was paralyzed in one leg due to the flames of war at the time. Mr. Kanh Khean recalled that during that regime, even though he was crippled, he was still forced to go to other places, such as closing the controversy.
Mr. Kanh Khean claimed that he, like other people, was very happy to hear about Samdech Techo Hun Sen's policy of national unification. Happy because the flames of war and misery under the leadership of the former Khmer Rouge leaders satiated the people.
Until the end of 1998, under the win-win policy set by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Anlong Veng district was liberated under the control of a single government. The people of Anlong Veng district also have the right to full freedom and the right to earn a living in accordance with the Constitution of the Kingdom of Cambodia, just like the general population.
The families of Prak Touch and Kanh Khean, despite being paralyzed in one leg, worked together to build a better family life after they were liberated from the control of former Khmer Rouge leaders. Today, her family has a fairly large house made of wood, the upper and lower parts made of stone. He also had two bulldozers and a shark camper for the ride.
