(Paris): Since mid-November, hot events erupted in France, starting with the increase in oil tariffs, which pushed up oil prices.
Here are the details of the protests in this Western European country:
Demonstration begins: November 17, 2018 * Reasons:: Increase of fuel tax, car tax and inappropriate action of the French President * Demonstration of the protesters: Reduce gasoline and car tax, raise the standard Of the people, oust President Emmanuel Macron and his government, stop his unpopular measures, and the government must be transparent and accountable to people at all levels
* Demonstration symbol: yellow vest
The reason why protesters use the yellow vest as a symbol is because the law since 2008 requires all drivers in France to wear a vest with a distinctive color on their car. As a result, this reflective vest has become a popular symbol of protesters as it is cheap, easy to buy and easy to spot.
That is why the anti-oil tax movement in France, dubbed the "Yellow Jacket Movement", began on Saturday, November 17, 2018 and then spread to neighboring Italy. Belgium and the Netherlands.
This is due to the increase in fuel prices, higher living costs. Demonstrators claimed that the burden of government tax reform fell on the shoulders of the working class and the middle class (especially those living in rural and suburban areas). The movement is noticeable in urban areas, but less active in rural areas.
Since the 1950s, the French government has strongly encouraged the production of diesel engines. Especially since 1980, Peugeot has been at the forefront of diesel engine technology. Incentives, combined with tax cuts for diesel carmakers, have boosted diesel production in France.
Unfortunately, the price of diesel in France increased by 16% in 2018, with gasoline and diesel taxes rising at the same time, plus an increase in taxes for 2019, making diesel as expensive as gasoline. Also.
* Economic reform
* Destruction of property
