The United States has become the largest exporter of oil for the first time in decades as global oil prices continue to fall.
The United States became the largest exporter of oil and petroleum products last week for the first time in decades, with a total of 211,000 barrels a day. But some experts say it is only a temporary result. This is according to a data quoted by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Friday afternoon, December 7, 2018.
For several weeks until Nov. 30, the United States imported 8.8 million barrels of crude oil and petroleum products a day, according to data released Thursday by the US Energy Information Administration (EIA). Out 9 million barrels per day. The EIA also said that the United States has become more of an oil importer than an exporter since 1973.
But by 2018, the United States imported a total of 3 million barrels of crude oil and petroleum products per day, up from 14 million barrels per day in 2005.
World oil prices fell on Thursday after the OPEC meeting in Vienna, Austria, ended without a decision on oil production cuts.
