Cambodian traffic police exchange experiences with Vietnamese traffic police to help reduce and solve traffic congestion in the city
(Hanoi, Vietnam): Cambodia's traffic police led by Lt. Gen. Run Rath Veasna, Director of the Traffic Police and Public Order Department of the National Police, paid a three-day official visit to Hanoi, Vietnam from March 3. Until December 7, 2018, the purpose is to meet with the Vietnamese traffic police and gain new skills for traffic management in Cambodia, especially in Phnom Penh.
For the meeting held on December 6, 2018, the Cambodian traffic police received the following important results:
* Traffic control by road safety camera system * Methods to reduce traffic congestion in the city * Traffic control at intersections * Methods to reduce traffic accidents * Traffic control of waterways and railways.
During the visit, the Cambodian Traffic Police also requested the Vietnamese Traffic Police to help build the capacity of Cambodian road, water, and railway traffic police officers.
(Phnom Penh): PRASAC, the leading financial institution in the field of microfinance and one of the five (five) major banking institutions in Cambodia, has supplied more than 70,000 books to its 30 libraries. Collaborate with the Curriculum Development Department of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports.
PRASAC has provided many reading books and facilities to the libraries in Preah Vihear, Siem Reap, Kampong Thom, Kampong Cham, Prey Veng, Kandal, Takeo, Kampong Speu and Battambang.
The books that PRASAC provides to primary, secondary and high schools are made in accordance with the standards of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, which to date have a total of more than 70,000 copies (seventy thousand copies) in both Khmer and English. Includes history books, dictionaries, social science books, Khmer literature, life skills books and story books.
Mr. Say Sony, Chief Marketing Officer, said that PRASAC's management and staff are very pleased to have contributed to the provision of reading books and library buildings to students in rural areas.
"We also receive applause from teachers, students, parents, the community and stakeholders, and support for creating a better reading environment with a rich library of books," he said. Each PRASAC. According to the survey, the average number of students reading books at each library is about 3,000 on a monthly basis.
