Dr. Richner's body was delivered to Siem Reap Airport at around 8 am, with a blessing ceremony dedicated to Dr. Richner's soul by about 700 monks.
His body was taken from the airport to some streets in Siem Reap before heading to Kantha Bopha Hospital in Siem Reap, where his body was to be stored.
The procession was opened by a siren car of a police officer and was accompanied by Samdech Chao Feang Kong Sam Ol, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Royal Palace, and accompanied by Pin Peat music.
According to Mr. Denis Laurent, on December 6, 2018, His Majesty the King, His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni and His Majesty the Queen Mother of Cambodia will present bouquets of flowers and relics at the shrine to dedicate. Funeral service for Dr. Beth Richner at the morgue.
Dr. Beth Richner, born in 1947 in Switzerland, died at the age of 71 on September 9, 2018 in Switzerland. The death of the father of Save the Children of Cambodia has deeply saddened the Cambodian people, as well as his virtuous deeds. Despite having no Khmer resin blood, Dr. Beat Richner spent almost half of his last life for Cambodian children.
Returning to the devastation of Cambodia due to the fires of war and the lack of space for pediatric treatment, Richner asked the Cambodian government to reopen and rebuild Kantha Bopha Hospital. The establishment of the Kantha Bopha Foundation in March 1992 took shape to begin reconstruction, and Kantha Bopha reopened in November 1992.
The five branches of Kantha Bopha Hospital have a total staff of 2,345, of which 1,749 are health workers and 1,242 are civil servants. In the last three years, Kantha Bopha Hospital has spent a total of more than 23 million US dollars per year. Of that, nearly $ 23 million was spent on staff, more than $ 14 million was spent on medicines and medical equipment, and about $ 2 million was spent on general operations.
