Mr. Vongsey Visoth gave a lecture on "Opportunities and Challenges in the Nation-Building Process" for Interns and Senior Officials at the Royal School of Administration
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of December 4, 2018, Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Vice Chairman of the Supreme National Economic Council, gave a lecture on "Opportunities and Challenges in the Construction Process." Nation ”to Interns, Senior Officials, Interns, Administrative Managers, Intermediaries, Intermediate Officers, Interns, Kramakars and senior officials at the Royal School of Administration.
HE Vongsey Visoth continued that the world as well as Asia is under four major trends:
* First: the polarization of the world * Second: to become the center of Asia's global economic growth, that is, Asia will have the highest economic growth, the largest economy and will become a production base and a place of Largest Consumption * Third: Industrial Revolution * Fourth: Climate change.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth continued that in order to seize the opportunity and solve the above economic challenges, the Royal Government of Cambodia has set out some important policies and strategies, including:
* First: Cambodia Industrial Development Policy 2015-2025 * Second: Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 * Third: Cambodia Vision 2050.
He stressed that Cambodia is very important to promote the implementation of the above policies and strategies, in which Cambodia must strive to achieve the maximum of the three goals of the Industrial Development Policy 2015-2025 and the strategic goals. 4 of the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 to build a base towards high-middle-income countries by 2030 and achieve the vision of high-income countries by 2050.
He said that in order to make Cambodia's vision for 2050 a reality and Cambodia can become a nation as strong and famous as during the Great Depression, the nation-building process must be based on five forces. Contains:
(China): UNESCO has included a traditional Taipei treatment method called the Intangible Cultural Heritage List.
Taipei's traditional healing practices by soaking in water are knowledge and rules related to life, health, treatment and prevention of diseases that Taipei people practice in the Taipei Autonomous Region of southwestern China.
In Taipei, the word "Lum" refers to the ancient knowledge and rules of how to dive in a natural hot spring or in water with leaves, plants or steam to keep the balance between body and mind. That people are healthy and cured of all diseases.
This ritual is consistent with the Jungwanga, or the five elements noted in ancient Taipei rituals, including earth, water, air, fire, and air.
Taipei's traditional healing practices by soaking in water have been included in the national list of elements representing China's intangible cultural heritage twice, once in 2008 and once in 2014.
Today, China has 40 items that have been added to the UNESCO list, including 32 regions and rules in the list of Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, seven intangible cultural assets that need urgent protection, and a program that must be protected. Selected to be included in the list of best preventative measures. (China's CCTV)
President of the Constitutional Council shares his condolences on the death of Mr. Prohm Nhean Vichet
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Im Chhun Lim, President of the Constitutional Council, expressed his deep regret and sadness over the death of Mr. Prohm Nhean Vichet, a former member of the Constitutional Council.
In his condolence letter to the family of the deceased on December 4, 2018, Mr. Im Chhun Lim considered the death of Mr. Prohm Nanavichet as the loss of his great husband, father and grandfather, who were pious and Dear family.
He also considered this death to be the loss of one of the nation's great dignitaries who had actively contributed to nation-building in the past.
The body of Mr. Prohm Nhean Vichet will be laid to rest in the traditional Buddhist ceremony at No. 1, Street 352, Sangkat Boeung Keng Kang I, Khan Chamkar Mon, Phnom Penh.
The family of the deceased invited relatives and friends of the deceased to attend the funeral as a farewell and a final farewell.
Mr. Prohm Nhean Vichet is a knowledgeable person and has worked for the benefit of the society. He served as Professor of Khmer and English Literature at Sihanoukville High School and Preah Suramarit High School between 1964-1970.
After Cambodia was liberated from the genocidal regime, he served as chairman of the editorial board of the Cambodian newspaper and previously served as undersecretary of state at the Ministry of Information in charge of the Information Center of the General Department of National Television of Cambodia (TVK) and General Department (AKP). . He also served as a lecturer at the Royal School and as a Special Task Force of the Royal Government.
From 1997 to 2002, Prohm Nhean Vichet was a member of the National Election Committee and chairman of the Standing Subcommittee on Media and Public Information Bureau. Between 2003 and 2004, he was appointed by the King as an Advisor to the Office of the Council of Ministers. Smart Axiata Awards Winners to 48 Outstanding Undergraduate Students in SmartEdu USDP
(Phnom Penh): After undergoing 13 days of training at SmartEdu University Student Development Program (USDP) in Siem Reap, three semi-finalists presented innovative solutions in the finals And the award ceremony at Sofitel Phnom Penh.
The closing ceremony was attended by Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports and Dr. Kan Chanmeta, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Posts and Telecommunications of Cambodia.
The winner of the finals will receive a cash prize of $ 1,800. The team that did the best during the workshop received a prize of $ 1,200. In addition, eight top students will receive a two-month internship with a two-month stipend at Axiata in Malaysia.
"The SmartEdu USDP program prepares Cambodian university students to lead the country's technology industry in the future and test their professionalism for real-world problem-solving skills," he said. I am very happy to spend some time with the participants in Siem Reap and I am very sure that the future of Cambodia will be bright. ”
Forty-eight candidates from 14 universities across Cambodia were shortlisted, with six candidates in each group. The teams then participated in a training program at the Sokha Hotel in Siem Reap, where they received theoretical, experimental, and personal development opportunities to develop communication skills, creative and critical thinking skills, as well as emotional management skills. .
Candidates participated in many competitions, workshops and experiments related to digital business, they were encouraged to express their abilities and give feedback to each other.
Ms. Keo Priyanith, a candidate from the Royal University of Phnom Penh, said that this training program is a first experience for most of us and it is very important to prepare students for the finals.
"The 13-day training program was busy and tiring, but I learned a lot about myself, my teammates and business leadership," Priyanith said. I would like to thank the committee that selected me to participate in this rare program to learn a lot of skills in this short time. I will use this knowledge and experience in my own studies and work in the future.
In addition, the SmartEdu Scholarship Program and the University Student Development Program (USDP) will continue to cultivate top human resources for Cambodia's digital and business sector, using tools and skills to help advance Cambodia's technology sector. A portion of the $ 1.5 million investment provided by Smart will be included in the national budget for capacity building and research and development.
