According to Ms. Lay Chanrasmey, the meaning of this traditional Khmer fashion includes:
1. The upper bouts featured two cutaways, for easier access to the higher frets. The lower bouts featured two cutaways, for easier access to the higher frets.
3. A pair of elephants represents power, strength, ambition and well-being, and also represents the wildlife and natural resources in Khmer territory, and especially the connection between the Khmer tribes during the Mountain Kingdom era.
4. The lower part is decorated with intricate patterns in the style of fairytale-era fairies carved on the walls of Angkor Wat and other temples of Khmer identity. The fairy represents a virgin and a heavenly woman, and this style also wants to show the progress of all fields of art, tradition, tradition and creative talent of the Khmer people in the Mohanakor era.
5. The jewelry is designed in true Khmer art and has a hairpin in the shape of a rumduol flower, representing the national flower of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
