Love inherited from Khmer ancestors! APSARA Authority experts teach conservation of temple stone sculptures to students of Banteay Samre Primary School
(Siem Reap): Dr. Ly Vanna, Director of the Department of Preservation of Temples in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology of the APSARA Authority, in collaboration with GIZ of Germany, organized a heritage education program for students of Banteay Samre Primary School in Angkor Park on the occasion of the anniversary. The 10th year of establishing a training program for Cambodian experts in the field of conservation and restoration of GIZ stone sculptures in the Angkor area.
The event took place on the morning of November 29, 2018, in the presence of Dr. Ly Vanna, Director of the Department of Temple Preservation in Angkor Park and Preventive Archeology, APSARA Authority's Stone Conservation Experts and two foreign technical facilitators. Three coaches, 50 students of Banteay Samre Primary School and 5 teachers.
The purpose of this program is to give some insights on the preservation of the stone sculptures through the live demonstration at Banteay Samre temple and to guide how to take care of the sculptures using materials and cleaning tools, etc. Apart from this, what is important for this program is to cultivate the idea of loving and participating in preserving the heritage of Khmer ancestors to the young students who live near the ancient temples.
In addition, Dr. Ly Vanna also reminded that in the Angkorian era, ancestors always reminded their children to love and take care of the temple and other ancient heritage through the inscriptions on the temple stone with prohibitions as well as cursing you. Whoever destroys the temple or commits inappropriate acts to the temple will go to hell until the moon, the sun does not rise.
