Mr. Hou Vuthy said that the procedure for recruiting new workers does not need to take as long as before, as long as the Malaysian side needs workers can be sent to work, while in the past workers have to have an employment contract and other formalities. Others can be sent for a long time.
Meanwhile, Malaysia has increased the wages of foreign workers going to work in Malaysia by more than $ 300 a month so that workers can earn a higher living wage and return home.
At the same time, Minister Ith Sam Heng requested that the three Malaysian recruitment agencies, in cooperation with the Cambodian Recruitment Agency, work together unanimously on the new working conditions. After the results, the technical working groups of the two countries will discuss these issues again before submitting to the two ministers for a final decision.
On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, Mr. Ith Sam Heng remains open to the terms of the Memorandum of Understanding on the Recruitment, Training, Transmission and Management of Cambodian Workers to Malaysia, which the two governments have agreed to. Already. It is an official legal document that becomes a framework for better cooperation in the field of labor.
He added that wages in Cambodia have also risen sharply, so Malaysia needs to benefit Cambodian workers through higher wages and better working conditions in order to attract Cambodian workers to work there. The countries that are the target of competition are also many, such as Korea, Japan, Singapore, Thailand and so on.
Please note that after the Royal Government of Cambodia has suspended the recruitment and sending of Cambodian workers to work in Malaysia, the Royal Government of Cambodia has established various mechanisms and legal standards to protect, manage and promote the rights of all Cambodian workers who are staying and working in Malaysia.
On December 10, 2015, the governments of the two countries signed two Memoranda of Understanding: Memorandum of Understanding on Recruitment and Use and Memorandum of Understanding on Recruitment and Use of Domestic Workers. .
Currently, there are 103 Cambodian private recruitment agencies licensed by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training to recruit, train, send and manage Cambodian workers abroad, including 69 companies in Malaysia (as of October 2018).
