Another rice exporter, representing more than 20,000 Cambodian farmers, has lodged a complaint with the European Union over plans to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice!
(Phnom Penh): Another new Cambodian rice exporter, representing more than 4,000 farming families, equivalent to 20,000 people, has filed a complaint with the European Union, which plans to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice.
The European Union will decide whether to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice on December 4, 2018.
The rice exporters' protest comes after the European Union's Ministry of Commerce began reviewing Cambodian rice following an Italian lawsuit and found that Cambodian rice had the potential to harm Italian farmers. It is unreasonable because Italian rice is a white rice country and Cambodian rice exported to Europe is a fragrant rice country that Italy does not grow anything to affect Italian rice.
"Italy's statement seems to contradict the reality in Cambodia in the letter I submitted: for him to reconsider the reality and for him (the European Union) to reconsider," he said. After he examined the realities that we produce that we have, and it is completely contrary to his findings, let him withdraw the tax barrier, our rice tax is inappropriate and not what he got. "Observe."
