(Siem Reap): Mr. Pak Sokhom, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Tourism, represented by Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism, presided over the opening ceremony of the Special Meeting of the ASEAN Tourism Monitoring and Supervision Commission (ATPMC) on the morning of November 28, 2018 at Angkor Paradise Hotel, Siem Reap Province.
The meeting was also attended by representatives of the 10 ASEAN member countries, hotel owners, guest house owners, trainers and national pillar evaluators of hotel services, spa and wellness business owners, technical staff and about 300 students.
The meeting will finalize the draft of the ASEAN Spa and Professional Training Standards (Spa Professionals), which Cambodia is responsible for developing the ASEAN Common Training Program Standards, as well as improving the training of spa and wellness professionals and providing additional skills. To spa professionals in line with national-ASEAN standards that meet common regional standards.
So far, the Ministry of Tourism has focused on training technical and hospitality skills for thousands of spa and wellness professionals in major tourist destinations to welcome national and international tourists by providing satisfactory services and codes. High professional ethics. The Ministry of Tourism has already developed and implemented its Cambodian standard of spa capacity and well-being through tourism vocational training schools and training programs.
The meeting was organized in accordance with the Strategic Plan for the Development of Human Resources in the Tourism Sector, which was set by the Ministry and promoted the training of new skills to those working in the spa and wellness services in accordance with ASEAN standards, both theoretical and practical. Direct application of existing skills aimed at achieving the following vision:
* By 2020, Cambodia Tourism will achieve 50% of the training and evaluation of Cambodian tourism professionals in accordance with national and ASEAN standards. * By 2025, Cambodia Tourism will achieve 100% of the training and evaluation of Cambodian tourism professionals by Participate in the campaign to promote "one employee, one skill." * By 2030, Cambodia Tourism will contribute to achieving the sustainable development goals of SDG 4 and DG8.
Recall that with the strong growth of national and international tourism in recent years, the tourism sector has created about 620,000 direct jobs, equivalent to 13% of total employment, and by 2020 and created nearly 1 million direct jobs. In 2025, there will be about 1.2 million people, and by 2030, there will be 2 million people, regardless of the indirect employment of tens of thousands more.
