(Phnom Penh): According to the latest World Bank Cambodia Economic Update released on November 28, 2018, Cambodia's economic growth rate is projected to be high. Over last year, an increase of 7.1 percent in 2018, led by domestic consumption and exports.
The report states that after a gradual economic recovery in the second half of 2017, Cambodia's exports grew rapidly, largely due to strong demand in the United States and the European Union. Cambodia is among the few countries in East Asia and the Pacific that are expected to experience modest economic growth.
Risks in the financial sector continue to increase, it will be more relevant to the construction and real estate sectors. External risks include the potential for the removal of preferential tariffs on Cambodian exports and the unpredictable effects of trade disputes between the United States and China.
"Many of the priorities for Cambodia include protecting the financial sector while building reserves, strengthening competitiveness and curbing exchange rate growth," said Ly Sideth, the World Bank's senior economist in Cambodia. Indeed, due to the rapid growth of imports recently.
