Spokesman Jin Malin: Foreign media with malicious intent to exaggerate the truth must be held legally responsible (Video Inside) (Phnom Penh): Chin Malin, Spokesman of the Ministry of Justice, cites foreign media based in Cambodia That is, located abroad and with malicious intent to spread exaggerated information in the sense of destroying or causing unrest in Cambodia as well as in the region must be held accountable under national and international law.
Mr. Chin Malin made this statement on the morning of November 24, 2018 during a roundtable discussion organized by the Royal Academy of Cambodia under the theme "Does Cambodia need the presence of foreign troops?"
The forum comes after the Bangkok-based Asia Times, which is suspected of being set up to serve the politics of a number of foreign groups, also used unidentified sources to accuse Cambodia of allowing China to set up military bases in Koh Kong province. It is also reported that Cambodia is at the center of the Cold War between the United States and China.
Spokesman Jin Malin said: "In connection with the responsibility of foreign media outlets not based in Cambodia, there are foreign-based media outlets that have published, misrepresented, misrepresented or intentionally misappropriated information. "Intentionally causing unrest in a country as well as in the region, and such a press as a principle of national law as well as international law must be held accountable."
According to Jean Malin, the responsibilities of foreign media are twofold: the professional responsibility of journalists and the legal responsibility.
He claimed that the exaggerated foreign media must be held accountable, but that it is up to the government to decide whether the government needs to be held accountable. But if the Royal Government of Cambodia wants foreign media, which is suspected of being set up to serve the politics of certain foreign groups, to be responsible, there must be adequate mechanisms and measures allowed by national law as well as international law.
Jean-Malin also pointed out the legal aspect, adding that the demand for professional responsibility of journalists is that those affected by the publication can demand a public apology and make corrections, but if not. Amendments can be made in accordance with international law, through administrative and diplomatic means, by requesting the government of the country where the newspaper is located to take legal action in its territory.
