According to ASEAN's official Facebook page, written in English and translated into Khmer, "Today, our Cambodian friends are starting to celebrate the Royal Water Festival, one of the most important festivals in the country. "Tens of thousands of Cambodians took a break to celebrate, including longboat races, concerts, fireworks and three-day gatherings."
Please be informed that the Royal Water Festival, floating lanterns, chanting and worshiping the moon god has existed since ancient times, which is mentioned in the documents of foreign historians, as well as in the inscriptions of Angkor temples. This Royal Festival is always held every year for 3 days, from the 14th to the 15th and the 1st of Roach Khek Tek on the Tonle Sap. For 2018, this Royal National Festival is held from November 21-23.
During the Royal Festival, there are also two other ceremonies, namely the boat race and the Ok Ambok festival to worship the moon god. The boat race lasts for three days, with lanterns floating at night, while the Ok Ambok festival to worship the moon god is held only on the second day, at midnight on the 15th day of the lunar month.
According to Dr. Chen Chanratana, President of the Khmer Heritage Foundation, the history of the Royal Water Festival, Ok Ambok, Floating Lanterns and Moon Worship is divided into three parts, including:
* Water Festival: Made to commemorate King Jayavarman VII and to commemorate King Ang Chan I defended the territory from enemy aggression.
* Lantern Floating Ceremony: To thank the Ganges and the earth and to pay homage to the glass jaws of the dragon world where the Buddha went to Stung Nam Tea and elsewhere.
* Ok Ambok and Moon Worship Ceremony: Made to commemorate the bodhisattva who referred to the birth as a rabbit, who at that time donated meat to Indra Kala himself as a foodless Brahmin.
