France issues statement welcoming historic convictions of former Khmer Rouge leaders Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of France has issued a statement welcoming the historic conviction of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia, backed by the United Nations, against former Khmer Rouge leaders Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan. Accused of genocide.
A statement from the French Foreign Ministry spokesman, obtained by Fresh News on the morning of 22 November 2018, stated: "France welcomes the historic decision of the Extraordinary Chambers in the A Cambodian court ruled on November 16 that genocide was committed by the Khmer Rouge against Vietnamese, Cham and other religious minorities.
France has supported the Extraordinary Chambers in the Kingdom since its inception in 2006 and will continue to support the work of this tribunal by contributing to the strengthening of international justice as well as the promotion of peace and the rule of law in Cambodia. "France reaffirms its commitment to the fight against impunity."
Nuon Chea, now 92, a former deputy secretary during the brutal Pol Pot regime and 87-year-old former head of state Khieu Samphan, has been charged with the genocide of the Cham Muslim and ethnic Vietnamese communities. April 1975 to January 1979.
The Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) convicted the two men of grave breaches of the 1949 Geneva Conventions and crimes against humanity in the form of murder, genocide. Enslavement, deportation, detention, torture, persecution for political, religious and racial and other inhumane acts against civilians in Cambodia during that time.
