Put Hean, a farmer in Chi Kreng district, was nicknamed Keo Sarath II by the villagers, intending to show his ability on television
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Keo Sarath, real name Chhum Tes, was born in Wat Kampheng village, Srong Battambang district, Battambang province. He is the second of seven children born to a father, Yat Chhum, and a mother, Krit Saran, who both died in 1977.
Keo Sarath was one of the most popular singers in the 1980s. In 1985, he started singing to raise money to support his family. He sang about 40 to 50 songs for Moyura Productions and then turned to songwriting and self-production in 1988, entitled The Golden Bell and the Golden Sparrow. The songs that made him famous everywhere were the song Sronod Dey Khmer, Pisak Deum Chhnang and so on.
On the other hand, Mr. Put Hean, 35 years old, is a farmer who is nicknamed by Mr. Keo Sarath II because his loud voice is similar to Mr. Keo Sarath's original voice. He was born in Thnal village, Lveang Russey commune, Chi Kreng district, Siem Reap province.
He said that he used to compete in the village song contest during the festival and always beat other contestants. He revealed that he also used to watch concerts at Bayon TV, but did not know what to do when he wanted to sing.
Mr. Theng Chak, a villager who lives more than 300 meters from Mr. Keo Sarath II, said that Mr. Put Hean really has a beautiful voice. He said that when there is a village exam, he always wins because there is a lot of support from people who know him.
