(Svay Rieng): Students as well as teachers of Andong Po Secondary School complained that one of the classrooms was made of rubber wood and was old, torn, difficult to study in the rain and lack of buildings. Study has just asked the provincial authorities as well as the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to provide a building with 6 more rooms after this secondary school has only one building.
The request for a school building of students as well as teachers was made on the morning of November 20, 2018 at Andong Po Secondary School in Trapeang Bos village, Andong Po commune, Romeas Hek district.
Mr. Prum Sophal, Director of Andong Po Secondary School, described that this secondary school has one building, which was built by the World Bank (ADB) in 2006. In 2016, while the number of students increased and the lack of any class, they were afraid to teach. During all the hours of the Ministry, he, as the Director, raised funds from philanthropists to build a theater 14 meters long and 7 meters wide for mobile hours (overlapping floors).
He added that so far, there are a total of 262 students, 142 girls and 3 children, with classes throughout the day, morning and afternoon.
Som Sovannath, a 13-year-old eighth-grade student living in Rong Snor village, Andong Po commune, Romeas Hek district, also complained that due to the lack of classrooms, teachers allowed their students to enter the mobile clock room. When it rains and the wind blows, the tables and chairs are very difficult to study because the factory is rotten and the rubber peels can be dangerous in any event.
The principal and teachers called on the head of the Royal Government and the provincial leaders of the Ministry of Education to help build a school building with 6 rooms to facilitate the study of students.
